A busy morning brings hundreds of reports about the same thing. We keep one ticket per issue
and merge every duplicate into it, so engineering sees one thread with a count.

## The rule

A report is a duplicate only when the evidence supports the **same cause**, under
[decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause), which replaces subject-only matching.

1. Read the body and look up the tenant locale, booking-form version, and report channel.
2. Confirm the mechanism: day-first app/email or booking-v2 parser swaps go to the [parser ticket](https://github.com/asledziewski/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1); booking-v3 hint/reader disagreement goes to the [hint ticket](https://github.com/asledziewski/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
3. Merge only when evidence agrees with that cause. Search by subject, but never decide by subject alone. Count each report ID once.

<Callout>
The same subject can describe different bugs. Different subjects can describe the same bug.
Tenant data determines the classifier's route; browser locale and intake tags can be wrong.
</Callout>

## Try it

<DedupeExplorer />

## When you are unsure

Keep the report separate and request the missing input, booking ID, timezone and timestamps.
One-day and timing anomalies need investigation even when the classifier returns parser.
Do not merge unknown tenants on subject alone. See the signed decision's unresolved-report checklist.
